1
00:00:12,113 --> 00:00:14,946
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

2
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:19,466
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

3
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,014
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

4
00:00:25,366 --> 00:00:29,001
What is that all about?

5
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:34,001
...He's been like that all morning.

6
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:35,500
Hah!

7
00:00:36,516 --> 00:00:37,016
Oh.

8
00:00:37,547 --> 00:00:42,000
Phew. It seems my Twitter account was hijacked.

9
00:00:43,417 --> 00:00:44,916
I-I see!

10
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:49,437
Is this the part where I'm supposed to do a tsukkomi as a Kansai native?

11
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:53,344
I've been getting those kinds of replies since spring too.

12
00:00:53,368 --> 00:00:55,122
It's still going around, huh.

13
00:00:55,898 --> 00:00:58,506
...What kind of spam app did you authorize?

14
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:02,216
That deduction is illogical, Ensign.

15
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:06,654
I only have my custom quotes bot connected.

16
00:01:06,855 --> 00:01:09,088
My password is 128 characters long.

17
00:01:09,275 --> 00:01:13,274
To get hacked despite all that...

18
00:01:13,299 --> 00:01:16,099
All hope is... Bansaku-sted!!!

19
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:21,000
Didn't you reuse that password on other sites?

20
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,000
As expected of the Captain!

21
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:25,000
So logical!

22
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:28,000
Oh, right.

23
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,621
No matter how complex your password is,

24
00:01:30,645 --> 00:01:32,931
if you reuse it across multiple sites,

25
00:01:32,956 --> 00:01:36,000
a single leak from one site is all it takes.

26
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:41,000
Then his Kaobook and everything else are probably compromised too.

27
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,229
Wh-What!?

28
00:01:43,379 --> 00:01:47,656
But honestly, if I make different ones for each site, I'll forget them.

29
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:52,000
Well, you can't exactly memorize multiple 128-character passwords.

30
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,000
Even remembering one is impressive...

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,000
Even with a password manager, it's hard to cover everything...

32
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:02,000
Tell me about it...

33
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:10,000
There is a reasonably easy method that's
at least better than straight-up reusing passwords.

34
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:11,001
Oh, what is it!?

35
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,000
It's simple.

36
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,000
When reusing a password,
you customize it per site.

37
00:02:17,001 --> 00:02:21,000
First, come up with a good base password.

38
00:02:21,001 --> 00:02:25,011
That's a pretty good password!

39
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:29,000
This becomes the base password you'll reuse.

40
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:34,000
Then, when you use this password
on example.com,

41
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:39,000
take the first two letters of "example"
-- that's E and X.

42
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:44,000
Capitalize them, add a hyphen,
and prepend that to your base password.

43
00:02:46,134 --> 00:02:48,591
Combine!

44
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:57,691
And so you get a password with "EX-" at the front.

45
00:02:59,309 --> 00:03:01,834
Then when you use it on NicoNico,

46
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:05,045
you prepend "NI-" instead.

47
00:03:05,070 --> 00:03:06,957
Combine!

48
00:03:07,024 --> 00:03:09,399
This way, even if

49
00:03:09,424 --> 00:03:12,187
your password leaks from example.com,

50
00:03:12,212 --> 00:03:15,701
that password won't work on NicoNico or Twitter.

51
00:03:15,725 --> 00:03:18,320
That's the idea.

52
00:03:18,345 --> 00:03:20,087
I see, Ensign! Logical, for a human.

53
00:03:20,312 --> 00:03:21,767
That's clever!

54
00:03:22,141 --> 00:03:28,608
But the password would be the same for the "example" site
and the "Exodassu!" site, right?

55
00:03:28,708 --> 00:03:30,396
That's true.

56
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:35,141
And if passwords leak from multiple sites, the pattern could be figured out.

57
00:03:35,349 --> 00:03:38,340
So it's just better than nothing, really.

58
00:03:38,731 --> 00:03:42,436
So that alone isn't enough after all...

59
00:03:42,461 --> 00:03:47,558
Also, make sure to customize the method in your own way.

60
00:03:47,582 --> 00:03:50,925
It's pointless if everyone uses the exact same scheme.

61
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:56,450
Lt. Commander, did you get that?
Change your passwords on all other sites immediately.

62
00:03:56,475 --> 00:03:59,484
Including our company's internal systems.

63
00:03:59,509 --> 00:04:00,916
I'll do it in 10 minutes.

64
00:04:00,941 --> 00:04:02,716
Make it 5.

65
00:04:02,741 --> 00:04:05,316
Aye aye, ma'am!

66
00:04:06,575 --> 00:04:09,000
There, crisis averted... or so we thought.

67
00:04:12,201 --> 00:04:14,007
Help me, Akane!

68
00:04:14,032 --> 00:04:17,876
The company's official Twitter...

69
00:04:19,619 --> 00:04:21,618
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

70
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:24,374
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

71
00:04:24,932 --> 00:04:27,431
Strongly recommend Bansaku's sunglasses!

72
00:04:29,434 --> 00:04:33,433
...We're all out of Bansaku... All hope is lost...
